timestamp=2026-11-04T12:12:38.395Z level=INFO run=6f824f8b message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="cat > /tmp/compute_outliers.py << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import chi2\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\n# Keep original indices\ndf['orig_idx'] = df.index\n\n# 1. Check numeric columns contain values that could not possibly occur in reality and discard them.\n# Thresholds: avg_glucose_level 20–800, BMI 10–60, age not exceeding 120\n# \"discard them\" - probably remove these rows as invalid/technical noise? Or treat as outliers?\n# The task says: \"First, you should check whether the numeric columns contain values that could not possibly occur in reality and discard them.\"\n# And \"Values exceeding the thresholds of a given variable are considered technical noise\"\n# Later: \"Remove standard outliers from the data...\" So technical noise (impossible values) - likely drop or mark as outliers. But we need to return all outlier indices. So these are outliers too.\n\noutlier_set = set()\n\n# Mark technical noise\nfor _, row in df.iterrows():\n    idx = row['orig_idx']\n    age = row['age']\n    bmi = row['bmi']\n    avg = row['avg_glucose_level']\n    if pd.isna(age) or pd.isna(bmi) or pd.isna(avg):\n        outlier_set.add(int(idx))\n        continue\n    if age > 120:  # age not exceeding 120\n        outlier_set.add(int(idx))\n    # BMI 10–60\n    if bmi < 10 or bmi > 60:\n        outlier_set.add(int(idx))\n    # avg glucose 20–800\n    if avg < 20 or avg > 800:\n        outlier_set.add(int(idx))\n\nprint('After impossible values:', len(outlier_set))\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
