timestamp=2026-11-04T12:13:30.956Z level=INFO run=6f824f8b message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="cat > /tmp/step3.py << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\ndf['orig_idx'] = df.index\n\noutlier_set = set()\n\n# 1. Technical noise\nfor _, row in df.iterrows():\n    idx = int(row['orig_idx'])\n    age = row['age']; bmi = row['bmi']; avg = row['avg_glucose_level']\n    if pd.isna(age) or pd.isna(bmi) or pd.isna(avg):\n        outlier_set.add(idx); continue\n    if age > 120: outlier_set.add(idx)\n    if bmi < 10 or bmi > 60: outlier_set.add(idx)\n    if avg < 20 or avg > 800: outlier_set.add(idx)\n\n# 2. Perform outlier detection using the 3*IQR method.\n# Apply to numeric columns as needed? The task says \"outlier detection using the 3*IQR method\" - generally on numerical variables. Also later says \"Remove standard outliers from the data...\" and then Mahalanobis on numerical variables. So probably apply 3*IQR on age, bmi, avg_glucose_level.\n\n# Work with a copy (clean view conceptually)\ndf_clean = df.copy()\n# But we need to identify outliers. Apply to all numeric cols mentioned: age, bmi, avg_glucose_level\n\ndef is_iqr_outlier(series):\n    q1 = series.quantile(0.25)\n    q3 = series.quantile(0.75)\n    iqr = q3 - q1\n    lower = q1 - 3 * iqr\n    upper = q3 + 3 * iqr\n    return (series < lower) | (series > upper)\n\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
