timestamp=2026-11-04T12:18:33.118Z level=INFO run=6f824f8b message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="cat > /tmp/build6.py << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import chi2\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\ndf['orig_idx'] = df.index\n\noutlier_set = set()\n\n# 1. impossible\nfor _, row in df.iterrows():\n    idx = int(row['orig_idx'])\n    age = row['age']; bmi = row['bmi']; avg = row['avg_glucose_level']\n    if pd.isna(age) or pd.isna(bmi) or pd.isna(avg):\n        outlier_set.add(idx); continue\n    if age > 120: outlier_set.add(idx)\n    if bmi < 10 or bmi > 60: outlier_set.add(idx)\n    if avg < 20 or avg > 800: outlier_set.add(idx)\n\n# 2. 3*IQR\nfor col in ['age', 'bmi', 'avg_glucose_level']:\n    s = df[col]\n    q1 = s.quantile(0.25); q3 = s.quantile(0.75)\n    iqr = q3-q1\n    if iqr == 0: continue\n    lo = q1-3*iqr; hi = q3+3*iqr\n    mask = (s<lo)|(s>hi)\n    for _, row in df[mask].iterrows():\n        idx = int(row['orig_idx'])\n        if col == 'avg_glucose_level':\n            val = row['avg_glucose_level']\n            same = df[df['avg_glucose_level']==val]\n            total = len(same); sc = (same['stroke']==1).sum()\n            pct = sc/total if total else 0\n            if pct >= 0.5:\n                continue\n            outlier_set.add(idx)\n        else:\n            outlier_set.add(idx)\n\n# 3 & 4\ndf_clean = df[~df['orig_idx'].isin(outlier_set)].copy()\nnum_cols = ['age', 'bmi', 'avg_glucose_level']\nfor c in num_cols:\n    mx = df_clean[c].max()\n    df_clean[c + '_norm'] = df_clean[c] / mx if mx != 0 else 0\ndf_clean['risk_proxy'] = df_clean['age_norm'] * df_clean['bmi_norm'] * df_clean['avg_glucose_level_norm']\n\n# 4. Identify outliers in the multidimensional space of numerical variables using the Mahalanobis distance.\n# Consider observations for which the squared distance exceeds the critical value of the chi-square distribution to be outliers.\n# The critical value for the chi-square distribution should be set to 0.01.\n# Variables: numerical variables - probably age, bmi, avg_glucose_level (or including risk_proxy? or just the base numerical features)\n# Multidimensional space of numerical variables - likely the 3 variables.\ndata = df_clean[['age', 'bmi', 'avg_glucose_level']].values\ncov = np.cov(data.T)\nmean = np.mean(data, axis=0)\ninv_cov = np.linalg.inv(cov)\nd2 = np.array([(x - mean).T @ inv_cov @ (x - mean) for x in data])\n# critical value at 0.01 significance level; df = number of variables\ndf_vars = data.shape[1]  # 3\ncrit = chi2.ppf(0.99, df_vars)  # critical value for alpha=0.01? The text says \"critical value for the chi-square distribution should be set to 0.01\" - ambiguous. Usually significance level alpha=0.01, so crit = chi2.ppf(1-alpha, df)\ncrit = chi2.ppf(1 - 0.01, df_vars)  # or maybe just use 0.01 as the threshold value directly? \"The critical value for the chi-square distribution should be set to 0.01\" - could mean set crit = 0.01. That’s unusual. Let’s check common phrasing. Or maybe the threshold parameter is 0.01 (alpha). Usually we compare squared Mahalanobis to chi2_{df, 1-alpha}. Maybe \"critical value ... should be set to 0.01\" means alpha=0.01, so threshold is chi2.ppf(0.99, df). That’s standard. Let’s go with that.\nprint('crit=', crit, 'df=', df_vars)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
