timestamp=2026-11-04T12:20:10.595Z level=INFO run=6f824f8b message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="cat > /tmp/check_cats.py << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\n# In the final step, you should also check whether there are any values among the categorical variables that are unlikely in reality,\n# such as “smoker” or “formerly smoked” / person working in children. Additionaly you should chceck\n# whether there are any toddlers that are students. If so they should be also considered as outliers.\n\n# Definitions given: \"For outlier detection in categorical variables, the age of children should be set to no more than 14.\n# For outlier detection in categorical variables, the age of toddelrs should be set to no more than 3.\"\n\n# \"person working in children\" - maybe \"children\" work type but they’re not children? Or people who work in children (work_type related)? Or perhaps the phrase means something like \"smoker / formerly smoked while being a child\"? Or \"person working in children\" as a work type? Or maybe the example given is a bit garbled - common phrasing: check for contradictions like smokers who are children? Or work_type \"children\" but age > 14? Also \"toddler\" defined as age <= 3.\n\n# Check cases\n# 1. Toddlers (age <= 3) who are students? \"whether there are any toddlers that are students. If so they should be also considered as outliers.\"\n# 2. Also the other examples: “smoker” or “formerly smoked” / person working in children - maybe children (work_type children or age <=14?) who are smokers/formerly smoked? Or other inconsistencies.\n\n# Let’s look at the exact text: \"values among the categorical variables that are unlikely in reality, such as “smoker” or “formerly smoked” / person working in children\"\n# Maybe a typo/formatting - could mean: e.g. someone who is a smoker/formerly smoked but also \"children\" (work_type) or child? Or children shouldn’t be smokers. Also \"working in children\" might refer to work_type involving children? Or maybe work_type is \"children\" (as in child labor?) - but dataset has work_type 'children'. Or perhaps just logical inconsistencies: e.g. work_type 'children' but age is adult? Or smoking status for very young children is odd. Also \"person working in children\" - maybe work_type 'children' is problematic? Or maybe it means if someone’s work is with children (e.g. teacher?) but combined with something else - not clear. Or look at the age-based definitions given in the same rule set.\n\n# Also separately: \"For outlier detection in categorical variables, the age of children should be set to no more than 14. For outlier detection in categorical variables, the age of toddelrs should be set to no more than 3.\"\n\n# So definitions: children are age <= 14, toddlers age <= 3. That gives us constraints.\n# Then check: toddlers (<=3) that are students -> outliers. Also check other inconsistencies like smokers/formerly smoked for children? Or work_type 'children' but age > 14 (child classified by work type but age doesn’t match)? Or vice versa?\n# Also \"smoker\" or \"formerly smoked\" / person working in children\" - maybe a child (age<=14) with smoking_status 'smokes' or 'formerly smoked' is unrealistic -> outlier.\n# That’s plausible.\n\nfor _, row in df.head(10).iterrows():\n    print(row['age'], row['work_type'], row['smoking_status'], row['stroke'])\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
